ediction markets pricing Conley near 65%. Lawler's big fundraising edge and cross-party endorsements keep this short of Likely D, and the sparse, stale, partisan polls add uncertainty.", "watch": "Fresh nonpartisan polling or the News 12 debate could shift the race either way, especially if Lawler's cash advantage or the carpetbagger attacks move voters.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 66 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Mike Lawler (R) is running."]}, "overview": "NY-17 pairs first-term incumbent Mike Lawler against Democrat Cait Conley in a Hudson Valley district that leans only slightly Democratic. Most expert raters call the race a Tossup, though several Democratic-leaning forecasters rate it a Lean D flip, and prediction markets uniformly give Conley about a 65% chance of winning. The Democratic fundamentals environment is strong this cycle.\n\nLawler holds a substantial money advantage, with more than $4.8 million on hand versus Conley's $1.3 million, and has drawn endorsements from some Democratic House members. The race has drawn heavy outside spending and national attention, including a Buttigieg rally for Conley. With limited and dated polling available, the race sits as a genuine tossup that leans narrowly Democratic.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7399, "margin": 4.04}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7185, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6936, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7094, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.6393, "margin": 3.0}]}